// target type is PARENTHESIZED. ParenConvT deliberately leaves out the bare-name and
// pointer types, which are the two shapes that are also EXPRESSIONS: `(f)(3)` has to
// stay a call of a parenthesized function value and `(*fp)(3)` a call through a
// dereferenced one. What is left - chan/map/func/struct/interface/slice/array - can
// never start an expression, so trying this first costs nothing and is unambiguous.
// (A parenthesized composite literal, `([]int{1, 2})`, still falls through: the ')'
